150 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 110 0 R
/E (Four elements of prior research are directly relevant to this study: the automated analysis of privacy-policy text, the measurement of consent interfaces, the measurement of tracking behaviour, and the emerging use of large language models to read policies at scale. Each of these fields in research has advanced considerably. However, what none has done, to our knowledge, is place the document and the behaviour side by side, on the same sites, and ask whether one can explain the other.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
151 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 110 0 R
/E (McDonald and Cranor's foundational estimate put the time an average user would need to read the policies of the sites they visit in a year at roughly 201 hours, with a national opportunity cost they valued in the hundreds of billions of dollars, this study was conducted in an era when a typical user visited far fewer sites than today [McDonald & Cranor 2008]. Subsequent work established that the problem has not eased. Analysing a corpus of thousands of policies from inside and outside the EU, Linden et al. found that policies grew markedly longer after the GDPR came into force, even as their coverage of GDPR-relevant topics improved [Linden et al. 2020]. A parallel line of work built the annotated corpora that made systematic policy analysis possible in the first place, most notably OPP-115, 115 policies segmented and labelled by legal experts [Wilson et al. 2016], and more recently GDPR-oriented taxonomies such as GoPPC-150 [Tang et al. 2024]. Our study takes the length and readability of policies as an input: we treat the policy as a claim to be tested against conduct, and our own reading-time figures update McDonald and Cranor's for a contemporary European sample.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
